<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Assiniboine</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Nakoda, Assiniboin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Assiniboine, or Nakoda, are a Siouan-speaking Indigenous people of the northern Great Plains, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saskatchewan">Saskatchewan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alberta">Alberta</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montana">Montana</a> and North Dakota. Having separated from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yanktonai">Yanktonai</a> Dakota, they became close allies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cree">Cree</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">fur trade</a> and in wars with the Blackfoot and Dakota. Today they live on reserves and reservations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/assiniboine.jpg" alt="Assiniboine" width="320" height="444" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Karl Bodmer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Assiniboin_indians_0065v.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name Assiniboine is the French rendering of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ojibwe">Ojibwe</a> asinii-bwaan, meaning stone Sioux. The people's own name is Nakoda, also written Nakota or Nakona. Their language belongs to the Mississippi Valley Siouan branch of the Western Siouan family. The Stoney of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alberta">Alberta</a> are closely related by language, and about 150 people speak Assiniboine today, most of them over forty.</p><p>Linguistic analysis shows the Assiniboine and Stoney sharing a common ancestry with the Sioux nation. Both Assiniboine and Sioux were gradually pushed westward from the woodlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minnesota">Minnesota</a> onto the plains by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ojibwe">Ojibwe</a>, who had acquired firearms from their French allies. The Assiniboine later obtained horses by raiding and trading with the Crow and the Sioux.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>At the height of their power the Assiniboine dominated territory from the North Saskatchewan River to the Missouri River, hunting bison on horseback. The first European to describe them was Henry Kelsey of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudsons-bay-company">Hudson's Bay Company</a> in the 1690s. They became important trading partners and middlemen in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">fur trade</a>, exchanging beaver furs and bison hides for guns, metal pots, wool blankets and glass <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a>. They belonged to the Iron Confederacy with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cree">Cree</a>, an alliance in the fur trade that also sheltered its members from the Blackfoot.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">Smallpox</a> and other Eurasian diseases brought epidemics with high mortality, and the Assiniboine population fell from about 10,000 in the late eighteenth century to about 2,600 by 1890. On 1 June 1873 American wolfers killed at least 20 Assiniboine in the Cypress Hills Massacre, which led to the formation of the North-West Mounted Police.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Traditionally the Assiniboine were semi-nomadic and followed the bison herds in the warmer months. Women gathered and cultivated plants, made clothing and tools, and cured meat and skins; men hunted, traded and made war. The Sun and Thunder were held to be the most important manifestations of the Great Spirit, and the people held the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-dance">Sun Dance</a> and sought guidance in vision quests.</p><p>Today Assiniboine people live on reserves and reservations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, often alongside other nations. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montana">Montana</a> these include the Fort Peck and Fort Belknap reservations. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saskatchewan">Saskatchewan</a> they include Carry the Kettle, White Bear, Ocean Man and Pheasant Rump; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manitoba">Manitoba</a> they hold no separate reserves.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Assiniboine” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Cree and the <b>Assiniboine</b>, or Nakoda, had long been allies, and in the 18th century they became the main middlemen between the Hudson's Bay Company posts on Hudson Bay and peoples of the interior.</blockquote><a href="/state/iron-confederacy">Iron Confederacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Riel surrendered and was hanged in Regina that November, and eight Cree and <b>Assiniboine</b> men were hanged at Battleford.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-government-of-saskatchewan">Provisional Government of Saskatchewan</a></li><li><blockquote>For a century the company kept to coastal forts such as York Factory, where Cree and <b>Assiniboine</b> traders brought beaver pelts in exchange for guns, kettles and cloth.</blockquote><a href="/state/ruperts-land">Rupert's Land</a></li></ul></section>
